WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:07.149
In 2013, Dark Horse Comics released an illustrated
version of George Lucas' original 1974 script

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:10.240
for a space opera called The Star Wars.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:15.540
The comic books gave series fans a glimpse
of how Lucas' original vision evolved into

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:17.200
what we saw on the screen.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.460
Some of the differences are staggering.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:26.020
In The Star Wars, Luke Skywalker is introduced
not as an idealistic young farm boy from Tatooine,

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:31.849
but rather as a grizzled 60-year-old general
on the planet Aquilae, one of the few planets

00:00:31.849 --> 00:00:33.960
not taken over by the Empire.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.690
He's a Jedi-Bendu, a member of a group of
warriors who were once the sworn defenders

00:00:38.690 --> 00:00:39.540
of the Emperor.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.560
The group has since defected. and its members
are being hunted by the Sith.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.520
Very few Jedi are left, and their numbers
are dwindling, just like they are in A New

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:49.020
Hope.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:54.440
The roles of our heroes shift around a bit
in this first draft, which introduces another

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.460
Jedi-Bendu by the name of Kane Starkiller.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.620
Kane lives on a desolate moon with his two
sons, Deak and Annikin.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:06.060
While he was born a human, he's mostly cyborg
by the time we meet him, left with nothing

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.060
but his original head and arm after a life
of brutal combat.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.420
After young Deak is killed by a surprise Sith
attack, Kane and Annikin flee to Aquilae so

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.020
that the brash young Annikin can complete
his Jedi-Bendu training with Luke.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:25.130
Shades of Anakin Skywalker's attitude from
Attack of the Clones are apparent here, but

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:30.140
instead of the Jedi braid, Annikin sports
a combination of a mullet and a man bun.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.440
It's not any cooler, it's just different.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.640
The villain known as Darth Vader is still
imposing in The Star Wars, but he's a lot

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:38.640
better-looking.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:43.520
While he bears some the scars of the Vader
we know, he has one robotic, red eye and a

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.760
full head of hair.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.010
In this version, he's a tactical commander
of the Empire.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:51.020
He has no special powers: he's neither Jedi
nor Sith.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.140
While he expresses some respect for Dark Side
users of the Force, his feelings quickly diminish

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.800
when they fail to get him instant results.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.420
This Vader occasionally wears the trademark
helmet, but only the top part.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:07.840
He doesn't need the rest of his suit, after
all: this interpretation comes from before

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.620
Vader was known to have a burnt-up backstory.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:15.500
But helmet or no helmet, he still looks like
he probably sounds like James Earl Jones.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:20.500
Princess Leia doesn't play the heroic role
here that she assumes in A New Hope.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.620
Instead, she's a college student who needs
to be rescued after war breaks out on Aquilae.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:31.099
Instead of A New Hope's Luke Skywalker, who's
a nice, sweet boy, she's retrieved by Annikin

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:36.200
Starkiller, an unlikable jerk who punches
her in the face when he needs her to cooperate.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.739
Despite these harsh beginnings, the two eventually
fall in love for reasons not even the script

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:41.520
can explain.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:46.880
When Leia's father is killed, she becomes
the queen of Aquilae. and the Empire zeroes

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.640
in on her as a target.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:52.240
Turns out, her planet possesses valuable cloning
technology.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.340
To get control of the planet and its lucrative
tech, the Empire sets out to capture her and

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.340
her siblings.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.019
Like a lot of material from this early script,
the name "Valorum" eventually showed up in

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.840
the movies, just in a different way.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:10.620
In The Star Wars, Prince Valorum is a powerful
Sith who's called in by the Empire for his

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.020
Jedi-hunting expertise.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.879
He's hired to capture the heroes, and though
he never quite succeeds, his mask makes him

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.500
look pretty badass even when he's failing.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:25.269
The Darth Vader we got in A New Hope is basically
a combination of The Star Wars' Vader and

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:25.800
Valorum.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.580
Originally, neither of those guys were that
effective as a villain.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.160
Mix the the two together, though, and the
result is iconic.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.900
The R2-D2 we know only speaks in beeps and
bloops, but in The Star Wars, he speaks in

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.080
full sentences, all while still looking like
a walking, talking trash can.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:48.760
Unlike his Astromech droid friend, C-3PO didn't
change much from his first appearances in

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:49.640
The Star Wars.

00:03:49.889 --> 00:03:55.719
In a way, that frustrating, snooty protocol
droid is one of this sprawling franchise's

00:03:55.719 --> 00:03:59.400
biggest constants, and for that, we salute
him.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.740
"I do believe they think I am some sort of
god."

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:08.440
One of the most dramatic changes from The
Star Wars to Star Wars proper is the appearance

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.880
of Han Solo's character.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:15.840
While his original version was still a morally
ambiguous scoundrel, he was also an enormous,

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:20.400
green, gilled creature, as opposed to a dashing
young Harrison Ford.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.450
An extra complication is that his particular
species was originally known for killing Wookiees,

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:30.130
not befriending them, making his eventual
partnership with Chewbacca all but impossible

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:31.620
in these early drafts.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.320
Even under his scaly skin, though, Solo seems
like pretty much the same guy.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:41.920
In The Star Wars, Wookiees basically serve
the same purpose that the Ewoks do in Return

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:42.700
of the Jedi.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:47.970
They're still 8-foot-tall killing machines,
but they're also very superstitious, like

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:48.800
the Ewoks.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:52.500
They also live in a society that looks a lot
like the Ewoks' village.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:57.800
In the original story, Annikin Starkiller
beats the Wookiee chief in combat and is subsequently

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:03.080
worshipped, a development that's ultimately
played for laughs in the movies by C-3PO instead.

00:05:03.140 --> 00:05:07.660
It's not too far off from the Wookiee society
we eventually learn about in Revenge of the

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:10.880
Sith, and, of course, the Star Wars Holiday
Special.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:15.620
Maybe it would've been cooler if Return of
the Jedi took place on Chewbacca's turf, following

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.700
an army of Wookiees instead of an Ewok insurrection.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:24.540
By the end of the script for The Star Wars,
Sith Prince Valorum and Jedi-Bendu Annikin

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:30.560
Starkiller actually team up to deal a blow
to the Empire, after Valorum decides Vader

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.720
is too disloyal to work with.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:38.160
Interestingly, Valorum has a lot of criticism
for Annikin over his love for Princess Leia.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:43.240
According to Valorum, emotions are a peculiar
weakness of the Jedi-Bendu, with such raw

00:05:43.240 --> 00:05:46.980
feelings supposedly being unbecoming of a
Sith.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.780
As the prequels eventually established, it's
supposed to be the Jedi who advocate shunning

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:57.060
one's emotions, while the Sith embrace their
feelings to become more powerful.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:02.860
Ultimately, after the Jedi teach the Wookiees
how to fly spaceships, the Death Star fortress

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:05.220
is destroyed, taking Vader with it.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:09.160
Aquilae is saved, along with Princess Leia
and her royal family.

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:13.870
Annikin Starkiller is given the job of being
the chief protector of the planet, and the

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:19.380
rest of the galaxy is at peace, with the Star
Wars in a cease-fire, at least for now.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:25.060
Overall, The Star Wars makes for a decent,
familiar story which is nonetheless too complicated.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:30.660
For better and for worse, many of the ideas
in Lucas' original script were saved and dispersed

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.700
throughout the Star Wars we know today.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:37.600
Can you really imagine this guy as a Wookiee-eating,
scruffy-looking swamp monster?

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:40.740
"Who's scruffy-looking?"

